WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.550
Imagine your body without a skeleton.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.950
You would be a helpless, floppy pile of tissues.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.750
Your skeleton gives you support and allows you to move.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.160
Cells also have similar needs,

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:12.890
and they too,

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:13.850
have a skeleton--

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.020
a cytoskeleton. A cytoskeleton,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.430
which is made of protein filaments,

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.050
is found in virtually all life.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:25.050
The cytoskeleton filaments form by a process called polymerization. 

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.900
Polymerization is similar to stringing small beads end to end to make a necklace.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.010
In the case of the cytoskeleton,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.940
the beads are individual proteins,

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.550
which associate end to end.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:36.720
However,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:37.830
unlike a necklace,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:38.950
which is always the same length,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.300
cytoskeletal filaments are dynamic.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.150
They grow and shrink by adding or losing proteins.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:49.150
This allows the cell to rebuild its cytoskeleton, positioning filaments when and where it needs it.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.750
Two types of cytoskeletal proteins are actin and tubulin.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.130
Actin polymerizes in a twisted pattern to make actin filaments. Tubulin 

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.280
polymerizes into microtubules,

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.700
which are shaped like cylinders.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.090
In addition to providing the cell with structure,

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.810
actin filaments and microtubules also act as roadways.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.300
Motor proteins walk along these roads,

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.550
carrying cargo to different parts of the cell and producing forces.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:16.820
For example,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:22.250
myosin is a motor protein that moves along actin and is the major force behind muscle contraction.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.440
In fact,

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:24.680
when you eat a steak,

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.490
the main proteins that you're eating are actin and mysoin.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:32.630
But what else does the cytoskeleton do? Microtubules grow and shrink to move 

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:37.270
DNA into two new cells when cells divide. Actin filaments grow and 

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.680
shrink to push cells forward as they crawl,

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.750
allowing cells, like white blood cells, to move through your body and fight invaders.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.550
And what about all the different beautiful shapes of cells in your body?

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.660
These are made possible by the underlying support structure,

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:51.850
the cytoskeleton.

